        "intro": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "",
            "body": "<p>Formaldehyde itself rarely appears on skincare labels today. However, some products contain preservatives that release small amounts of formaldehyde, which can cause allergic reactions in susceptible individuals. These ingredients are particularly important to recognize if you have a confirmed formaldehyde allergy.</p>",
            "size": "large"
          }
        },
        "divider": {
          "type": "divider",
          "settings": {}
        },
        "what_is_a_formaldehyde_r": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What is a formaldehyde releaser?",
            "body": "<p>Formaldehyde releasers are preservatives that break down over time and release small amounts of formaldehyde into products. Manufacturers use them to keep bacteria and mold out of water-based products like shampoos. Formaldehyde has been used to preserve cosmetics for about a century, largely because it is cheap and effective, and it still turns up in some <a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics/cosmetic-products/nail-care-products\">nail hardeners</a> and <a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics/cosmetic-products/hair-smoothing-products-release-formaldehyde-when-heated\">hair-smoothing treatments</a>.</p>",
            "size": "regular"
          }
        },
        "are_formaldehyde_release": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Are formaldehyde releasers bad for eczema?",
            "body": "<p>For sensitive skin, they can be. Formaldehyde is a well-established contact allergen. The American Contact Dermatitis Society named it the 2015 Contact Allergen of the Year, and both formaldehyde and quaternium-15 sit on the <a href=\"https://www.contactderm.org/UserFiles/file/American_Contact_Dermatitis_Society_Core_Allergen.2-1_v1.pdf\">standard allergist patch-test panel</a>. If a patch test shows that you are allergic to formaldehyde, your dermatologist or allergist may recommend avoiding formaldehyde-releasing preservatives as well, depending on your individual sensitivities. If you think a preservative is behind a reaction, ask a dermatologist or allergist about patch testing.</p>",
            "size": "regular"
          }
        },
        "quote": {
          "type": "quote",
          "settings": {
            "quote": "\"Formaldehyde is the American Contact Dermatitis Society Contact Allergen of the Year for 2015. The exposure is widespread, and contact allergy might be difficult to suspect in the individual dermatitis patient.\"",
            "citation": "Pontén & Bruze, Dermatitis, 2015",
            "citation_link": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25581665/"
          }
        },
        "what_are_the_symptoms_of": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What are the symptoms of a formaldehyde allergy?",
            "body": "<p>A formaldehyde allergy is a form of <a href=\"/pages/contact-dermatitis-1\" title=\"allergic contact dermatitis\">allergic contact dermatitis</a>. According to DermNet, it causes an <a href=\"https://dermnetnz.org/topics/allergic-contact-dermatitis\">itchy, red, scaly rash that can blister and weep</a>, usually 24 to 72 hours after contact. It often shows up in one area, such as the face or hands, but it can also be widespread. Because the rash shows up a day or more after contact, the cause can be hard to suspect.</p>",
            "size": "regular"
          }
        },
        "which_ingredients_releas": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Which ingredients release formaldehyde?",
            "body": "<p>These are the names to look for on a label.</p>",
            "size": "regular"
          }
        },
        "label_check": {
          "type": "callout",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Label check",
            "body": "<ul><li>Quaternium-15</li><li>DMDM hydantoin</li><li>Diazolidinyl urea</li><li>Imidazolidinyl urea</li><li>Bronopol (2-bromo-2-nitropropane-1,3-diol)</li><li>Sodium hydroxymethylglycinate</li></ul>"
          }
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "",
            "body": "<p>These ingredients turn up more often in rinse-off products like shampoos, body washes and liquid soaps, but some creams and lotions use them as well. A \"gentle\" or \"hypoallergenic\" label does not rule them out, so read the ingredient list itself.</p>",
            "size": "regular"
          }
        },
        "where_else_is_formaldehy": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Where else is formaldehyde found?",
            "body": "<p>Outside skincare, formaldehyde turns up in household detergents, air fresheners, metal polishes, paints, glues and varnishes, and in resins that bind pressed-wood materials like chipboard and plywood. Some clothing and textiles are treated with formaldehyde resins. It is so common in the environment that it can be hard to avoid completely.</p>",
            "size": "regular"
          }
        },
        "what_should_i_do_if_i_re": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What should I do if I react?",
            "body": "<p>Stop using the product you suspect and see a dermatologist or allergist. Patch testing can confirm the allergy, and formaldehyde and some releasers may need to be tested separately to get a clear answer. If clothing seems to be involved, machine-wash new garments before wearing them.</p>",
            "size": "regular"
          }
        },
        "which_exederm_products_a": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Which exederm products are free of formaldehyde releasers?",
            "body": "<p>exederm products are made without formaldehyde and formaldehyde releasers. Our washes and haircare, including exederm <a href=\"/products/eczema-wash\" title=\"Eczema Body Wash\">Eczema Body Wash</a>, <a href=\"/products/eczema-shampoo\" title=\"Eczema Shampoo\">Eczema Shampoo</a> and <a href=\"/products/baby-eczema-wash\" title=\"Baby Eczema Wash\">Baby Eczema Wash</a>, are preserved with phenoxyethanol and caprylyl glycol instead, and the full ingredient list is on every product page. The full exederm line holds the National Eczema Association Seal of Acceptance.</p>",
            "size": "regular"
          }
        }
